<header><p class="st-kicker">crusader state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Caesarea</h1><p class="st-meta">1101 CE – 1265 CE · capital Caesarea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Crusader lordship on the coast of Palestine whose 13th-century walls still stand at Caesarea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Baldwin I took Caesarea in May 1101 with a Genoese fleet, which received a share of the spoils including a green glass bowl believed to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacro-catino">Sacro Catino</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>. The city became the seat of a lordship, held from about 1110 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eustace-grenier">Eustace Grenier</a> and his descendants.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Caesarea was one of the major lordships of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> and the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> archbishop. Its lords, the Grenier family and later the Aleman line, lived from the harbour and the coastal plain. The town was smaller than the Roman city, but its defences were rebuilt several times, most thoroughly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX</a> in 1251-1252 with a sloping glacis and moat.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took Caesarea in 1187; it was regained in 1191. In 1265 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> captured it after a short siege and razed its walls; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-aleman">John Aleman</a> was its last lord.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-caesarea.jpg" alt="Lordship of Caesarea" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Royaume_de_J%C3%A9rusalem.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1101 CE – 1265 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>crusader state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caesarea</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eustace Grenier, John Aleman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Caesarea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1183&amp;focus=lordship-of-caesarea" aria-label="Lordship of Caesarea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5576 996 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Caesarea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5896 1252L5908 1277M5896 1252L5815 1335"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0M5815 1335h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5896 1252h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5896 1252h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Caesarea. <a href="/atlas?year=1183&amp;focus=lordship-of-caesarea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

